#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# protect

#% text
# `protect(f, ...)` gives the symbols the attribute `protected`: their definitions may no longer change.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The value is the list of the names, as strings, of the symbols that were not protected before.

#% item
# The arguments are not evaluated; a list of symbols, or a string naming a symbol, also works.

#% item
# Assigning to a protected symbol, defining a rule for it, attaching a rule to it with `@on`, `unset` and changing its attributes give `failure(protected, f)`. The symbol's existing definitions keep working.

#% item
# `unprotect(f)` or `clear_attributes(f, protected)` removes the attribute.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
rule area(r_) := pi*r^2
protect(area)
#% output
#= ["area"]

#% cell
attributes(area)
#% output
#= [protected]

#% cell
rule area(r_) := 2*r
#% output
#! failure(protected, area)

#% cell
area(2)
#% output
#= 4*pi

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Own values:

#% cell
limit_value = 10
protect(limit_value)
limit_value = 20
#% output
#! failure(protected, limit_value)

#% cell
unprotect(limit_value)
limit_value = 20
limit_value
#% output
#= 20

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `unprotect`, `attributes`, `unset`
